  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "\"Cyber\" relates to computers, networks, and the internet, originating from the Greek kybernan (to pilot). It covers security, digital forensics, and AI, facing threats like phishing and ransomware. High-demand careers include information security analysts, while key challenges involve defending against advanced, AI-driven attacks.Key Aspects of Cyber TechnologyDefinition & Origin: Refers to anything related to computers, information technology, and the internet. The prefix originates from the Greek verb kybernan, meaning \"to pilot\" or \"to steer,\" which also gives us the word \"cybernetic\".Types of Cyber Threats: Common attacks include phishing (disguised emails), malware, ransomware (holding data for money), and Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks. Emerging threats include AI-enabled hacks, which make attacks more sophisticated.Applications & Scope: Cybersecurity spans critical infrastructure protection (power plants, finance), network security, and data protection. It also covers cyber-warfare, which integrates digital intelligence and operations with traditional military functions.Cybersecurity Challenges: Defending infrastructure, preventing data theft (which causes over $16 billion in losses, per the FBI's 2025 Internet Crime Report), and keeping pace with AI-driven malicious tools. Small businesses are increasingly vulnerable as they often lack advanced tools.Career Opportunities: Roles in high demand include information security analysts, cyber threat intelligence analysts, and security engineers, typically requiring technical education in computer science. Programs like CYBER.ORG provide education to build these pathways.Cybersecurity Action StepsIf you suspect a cyberattack, follow guidance from Ready.gov to secure accounts and report to the FBI Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3).",
